research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-6-{"id":"lem-every-integer-above-one-has-a-prime-divisor","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Abstract_and_Geometric_Algebra/Rings_with_Inquiry_%28Janssen_and_Lindsey%29/01%3A_The_Integers/1.03%3A_Primes_and_Factorization"],"rationale":"The source states the same existence theorem. The item supplies the least-divisor proof and spells out the divisor set and its least-element contract.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-7-{"id":"thm-prime-factorisation-exists","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math-cs.gordon.edu/ntic/ntic2020/section-fta.html"],"rationale":"The source proves existence of a finite prime product for positive integers. The item recasts it as an indexed-list theorem, includes n=1 as the empty product, and adds the converse range clause.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-8-{"id":"thm-fundamental-theorem-of-arithmetic","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://web.math.utk.edu/~dcartwr1/iaawa/section-division-algorithm.html"],"rationale":"The source gives existence and uniqueness up to rearrangement. The item expresses rearrangement as a permutation of finite von Neumann-indexed lists and treats the empty list explicitly.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-9-{"id":"cor-factorisation-of-a-nonzero-integer","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://web.math.utk.edu/~dcartwr1/iaawa/section-division-algorithm.html"],"rationale":"The source states the same signed theorem for every nonzero integer, with a unit u=±1 and uniqueness of the unit and prime list up to rearrangement. The local proof is dependency-indexed.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:10:{"id":"thm-euclid-infinitude-of-primes","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://web.math.utk.edu/~dcartwr1/iaawa/section-division-algorithm.html"],"rationale":"The source gives Euclid's product-plus-one argument and infinitude conclusion. The item strengthens the presentation to every finite prime list and formalizes nonfiniteness by excluding an equinumeration with a natural; the proof adapts the classical construction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-11-{"id":"def-p-adic-valuation","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/VIGRE/VIGRE2011/REUPapers/Herwig.pdf"],"rationale":"The source defines the exponent of p in a nonzero integer as the greatest exponent for which the prime power divides it. The item constructs the maximum from boundedness and deliberately leaves v_p(0) undefined rather than adjoining infinity.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-12-{"id":"lem-p-adic-valuation-basic","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/VIGRE/VIGRE2011/REUPapers/Herwig.pdf"],"rationale":"The source's integer valuation decomposition yields divisibility through the valuation, extraction of p^v with p-free cofactor, and the unit and prime values. The item separates these consequences and proves its local boundary conventions.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-13-{"id":"lem-p-adic-valuation-additive","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/VIGRE/VIGRE2011/REUPapers/Herwig.pdf","https://www.math.toronto.edu/~ila/Neukirch_Algebraic_number_theory.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state multiplicative additivity and the ultrametric lower bound. The item restricts the integer valuation to nonzero inputs and makes the nonzero-sum and natural-valued conventions explicit.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
