research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-21-{"id":"ex-no-rational-square-root-of-three","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.wichita.edu/~hammond/class-notes/section-logic-proofs.html","https://users.math.msu.edu/users/magyarp/math310/","https://www.d.umn.edu/~jgreene/Number_Theory/Homework/HW_3_sol.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources establish irrationality of sqrt(3), the general non-square square-root criterion covering sqrt(6), and irrationality of the cube root of 2. The item conjoins the three claims in quotient-field notation and discharges them through its earlier rational-root corollary.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-22-{"id":"ex-arbitrarily-long-runs-of-composites","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.hmc.edu/funfacts/gaps-in-primes/"],"rationale":"The source constructs arbitrarily long consecutive composite runs as N!+2 through N!+N. The item uses the semantically identical finite product 2·3···(n+1), shifts the indexing, and includes n=0.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-23-{"id":"cex-hilbert-monoid-factorisation-not-unique","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://vadim.sdsu.edu/intro-factorization.pdf"],"rationale":"The source defines Hilbert monoids H_m={x≥1:x≡1 mod m} and records failure of unique factorisation in arithmetic congruence monoids. The item specializes to H_3 and supplies the elementary irreducible witness 100=4·25=10·10.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-24-{"id":"cex-calling-one-prime-destroys-uniqueness","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Abstract_and_Geometric_Algebra/Rings_with_Inquiry_%28Janssen_and_Lindsey%29/01%3A_The_Integers/1.03%3A_Primes_and_Factorization"],"rationale":"The source gives the same failure: if 1 is prime, 6 has the factorizations 2·3, 1·2·3, 1·1·2·3, and so on. The item formalizes this as unequal list lengths under the modified definition.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:25:{"id":"fs-euclids-product-of-primes-plus-one-is-prime","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://homepage.iis.sinica.edu.tw/~bywang/courses/discrete-math/note-3.pdf"],"rationale":"The source states the same false conjecture and exact counterexample 1+2·3·5·7·11·13=30031=59·509. The item expands the arithmetic and composite check in library notation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-26-{"id":"fs-n-squared-plus-n-plus-forty-one-is-always-prime","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.purdue.edu/~catlind/2017MA341/MA341-Lecture2.pdf"],"rationale":"The source refutes the reindexed form n^2−n+41 at n=41 by 41^2=1681. Replacing its n by n+1 gives the item's n^2+n+41 counterexample at n=40; the proof expands that substitution.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
research/audit/wave1-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-27-{"id":"fs-every-fermat-number-is-prime","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://sites.millersville.edu/bikenaga/number-theory/fermat-numbers/fermat-numbers.html"],"rationale":"The source states Fermat's conjecture, gives F_5=2^32+1, and proves 641 divides it from 641=2^4+5^4=5·2^7+1. The item reparameterizes the Fermat numbers by the power-of-two exponent and rewrites the sourced computation without congruence notation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-03"}
