research/audit/wave0-abstract-algebra.provenance.jsonl-21-{"id":"lem-intersection-of-subgroups","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subgroup"],"rationale":"Sourced: \"The intersection of subgroups A and B of G is again a subgroup of G\". Delta, and a strengthening in the safe direction: the item states it for an arbitrary NONEMPTY family and flags the nonemptiness hypothesis as load bearing (the empty intersection is not a subset of G under the conventions used here). Proof written locally.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-abstract-algebra.provenance.jsonl-22-{"id":"def-generated-subgroup","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generating_set_of_a_group","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclic_group"],"rationale":"Sourced: <S> as \"the smallest subgroup of G containing every element of S\", equivalently \"the intersection over all subgroups containing the elements of S\", and the definitions of cyclic subgroup and cyclic group. The item takes the intersection form as primary and derives the smallest-subgroup property; it also derives <empty> = {e} rather than stipulating it.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-abstract-algebra.provenance.jsonl-23-{"id":"lem-cyclic-subgroup-is-the-set-of-powers","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclic_group","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generating_set_of_a_group"],"rationale":"Sourced: \"<g> = { g^k | k in Z }\" and \"Every cyclic group is abelian\". The item merges the two into one lemma and proves both inclusions separately. Proof written locally.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-abstract-algebra.provenance.jsonl:24:{"id":"thm-division-algorithm-in-z","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_division","https://terrytao.wordpress.com/books/analysis-i/"],"rationale":"Sourced: \"Given two integers a and b, with b != 0, there exist unique integers q and r such that a = bq + r, and 0 <= r < |b|\". Convention delta recorded: the item SPECIALISES to b > 0 with 0 <= r < b, because no absolute value on Z exists at this point in the library reading order, and it also defines b | a for use on the page. A specialisation with unchanged content, hence ai-altered. Proof written locally from the ordered-ring axioms and well-ordering.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-abstract-algebra.provenance.jsonl-25-{"id":"lem-order-of-element-exists","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_(group_theory)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pigeonhole_principle"],"rationale":"Sourced: in a finite group every element has finite order, i.e. some positive power is the identity. Delta: the item proves it by applying the sharpest pigeonhole claim directly to k |-> beta(g^k) from sigma(m) to m, deliberately avoiding \"a subset of a finite set is finite\", which is not yet available; the statement itself is the standard one. Proof written locally.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-abstract-algebra.provenance.jsonl-26-{"id":"def-order-in-a-group","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_(group_theory)"],"rationale":"Sourced: |G| as the number of elements of a finite group, and ord(a) as \"the smallest positive integer m such that a^m = e\", infinite otherwise. Convention deltas recorded: |G| is the unique von Neumann natural equinumerous with G (uniqueness discharged by the pigeonhole lemma), min S_g is supplied by the well-ordering principle, and the symbol infinity is reserved and carries no arithmetic.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-abstract-algebra.provenance.jsonl-27-{"id":"lem-order-characterisation","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_(group_theory)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclic_group"],"rationale":"Sourced: \"a^k = e if and only if ord(a) divides k\" and \"ord(a) = ord(<a>)\". The item merges these with distinctness of g^0,...,g^{n-1} and the infinite-order dichotomy into one lemma, and states the divisibility clause over integer exponents. Proof written locally, driven by division with remainder.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
